<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">James Butler, 1st Duke of Ormonde</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1610">1610</a>–<a href="/years/1688">1688</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as James Butler, 12th Earl of Ormond, James Butler, 1st Duke of Ormond, 1st Duke of Ormond</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">James Butler, 1st Duke of Ormonde, was an Irish nobleman who led the royalist side in Ireland in the wars of the 1640s and went into exile after Cromwell's conquest. He served as Lord Lieutenant of Ireland under both Charles I and Charles II, and was made a duke in 1661 after the Restoration. He was among the most powerful men in Ireland of his century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/james-butler-1st-duke-of-ormonde.jpg" alt="James Butler, 1st Duke of Ormonde" width="320" height="396" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: anonymous · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Peter_Lely_(1618-1680)_(style_of)_-_James_Butler_(1611%E2%80%931688),_1st_Duke_of_Ormonde,_in_Garter_Robes_-_1171123_-_National_Trust.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>James Butler was born on 19 October 1610 at Clerkenwell in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, the eldest son of Thomas Butler, Viscount Thurles, and his English Catholic wife Elizabeth Pointz. His father died in a shipwreck off <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglesey">Anglesey</a> in 1619, and in 1623 King James I made the boy a ward of Richard Preston, Earl of Desmond, and placed him with George Abbot, Archbishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canterbury">Canterbury</a>, to be brought up a Protestant. Unlike almost all his Butler relatives he remained one. At <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christmas">Christmas</a> 1629 he married his cousin Lady Elizabeth Preston, Desmond's heiress, ending a long quarrel between the families.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Having succeeded his grandfather as 12th Earl of Ormond, he became the chief supporter of Thomas Wentworth, later Earl of Strafford, and in 1640 was made commander-in-chief of the forces in Ireland. After the rebellion of 1641 he cleared the country around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dublin">Dublin</a>, lifted the siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/drogheda">Drogheda</a> and won the Battle of Kilrush, and he was created Marquess of Ormond on 30 August 1642. In 1643 he agreed a ceasefire with the Catholic Confederation and sent troops to fight for Charles I in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, and he was sworn in as Lord Lieutenant on 21 January 1644.</p><p>The Confederates rejected his First Ormond Peace of 28 March 1646, and in 1647 he handed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dublin">Dublin</a> to the Parliamentarians. Returning in 1648, he made peace with the Confederates on 17 January 1649 and commanded their armies with the Royalists, but he was routed at the Battle of Rathmines in August 1649 and could not stop the Cromwellian conquest. Ousted from command late in 1650, he spent the following years in exile with Charles II, his Irish lands confiscated.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>After the Restoration he was created Duke of Ormond on 30 March 1661 and became Lord Lieutenant again, passing the Act of Explanation in 1665. Removed in 1669, he was elected Chancellor of the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxford">Oxford</a>, escaped an attempt by Thomas Blood to carry him off and hang him in December 1670, and served once more as Lord Lieutenant from 1677, his last act being to proclaim James II in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dublin">Dublin</a>. He died at Kingston Lacy in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dorset">Dorset</a> on 21 July 1688 and was buried in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/westminster-abbey">Westminster Abbey</a>; his grandson James succeeded as 2nd Duke.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “James Butler, 1st Duke of Ormond” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Confederation dissolved itself in 1649 to ally with the royalists under <b>Ormond</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/confederate-ireland">Confederate Ireland</a></li><li><blockquote>Thomas Butler, the 10th Earl, helped crush the Desmond rebellions; <b>James Butler, 1st Duke of Ormonde</b>, was Lord Lieutenant under Charles II.</blockquote><a href="/state/earldom-of-ormond">Earldom of Ormond</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Clerkenwell, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. Died in Kingston Lacy, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.53,-0.1&amp;year=1660" aria-label="Clerkenwell on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of James Butler, 1st Duke of Ormonde"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4995 5722h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4995 5722h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=51.53,-0.1&amp;year=1660">What was here in 1660? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
